WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.706 --> 00:00:02.476
(air whooshing)

00:00:02.476 --> 00:00:06.226
(inspirational spacey music)

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.140
- Today we're gonna look at a demo

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.230
of our project Feature Fields
for Robotic Manipulation.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.930
- The purpose of this project
is to give these robots

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.630
the ability to understand the world in 3D.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.130
- [William] We're going to get the robot

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.930
to grasp these objects on the table

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.640
using open-ended language.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.840
So what the robot's doing now,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.630
is it's picked up this camera
mounted on a selfie stick

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.080
and it's going to take
50 RGB images of a scene.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.670
What this gives us is a holistic

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.130
understanding of the entire scene.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.810
- [Ge] So neural radiance field is a way

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.140
to turn these images into
a 3D model of the scene

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.620
that can render hyper
photorealistic renders of the scene.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.930
- And in addition to just
the neural radiance fields,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.480
we also train a 3D feature field.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.550
And this 3D feature field lifts features

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.440
from 2D Vision foundation models,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.510
which are trained on
internet scale data sets

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.820
into a 3D feature representation.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.410
- [Ge] The robot hasn't seen these objects

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.820
before during training.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.260
In fact, the robot doesn't even know

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.290
which object we're gonna ask
it to pick up until we tell it

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.420
so this is what makes
this task open-ended.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.790
- Here we've exported a
point cloud of the scene

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.950
using the neural radiance
field we just trained.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.140
As you can see, we can go from 2D images

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.850
to this 3D representation.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.200
So now let's ask the robot

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.870
to grasp specific objects via language.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.130
Here you can see the grasps

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.870
overlaid on top of that point cloud.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.000
And now we can ask the
robot to find a motion plan

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.560
to execute on the robot.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.200
Here's a visualization of what
we expect the robot to do,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.640
which is pick up Baymax

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.520
and can see the robot's
finding a path to grasp Baymax,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.590
pick it, and then place it into the bin

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.840
on the other side of the table.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.990
Now let's see the robot
planning to grasp the bowl,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.430
and the important thing is that the robot

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.190
has never seen how to grasp a bowl before.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.980
It's going to pick it and
then place it into the bin.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.270
- [Ge] Now we're gonna test if the robot

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.530
can pick up the spatula.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.270
The spatula's a pretty difficult object

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.030
because it's held as a very awkward angle

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.480
and it's also very thin,
it's difficult to grasp.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.730
So what you just saw,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.000
is the robot being able to
pick up all of these objects

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.940
despite that it has never
seen these objects before.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.320
- And in the future, we really want

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.660
to make this representation more real-time

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.046
so we can enable robots
to do more dynamic tasks

00:02:18.046 --> 00:02:20.430
in the household, in the factory,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.130
and in manufacturing environments.

